cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  CleitonForge: differential testing for quantum compilers, with declared
  oracle power
message: >-
  If you use this software, or the oracle-power results in research/,
  please cite it using these metadata.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Cleiton Augusto
    family-names: Correa Bezerra
    email: augusto.cleiton@gmail.com
    city: Porto Velho
    country: BR
repository-code: 'https://github.com/cleitonaugusto/CleitonForge'
abstract: >-
  A differential fuzzer for quantum compilers, and a measurement of what its
  oracles can actually detect. A negative result in differential testing has no
  meaning without a declared oracle power, and this repository measures that
  power rather than asserting it.

  Two blindness results are established. First, an outcome-counts oracle has
  provably and empirically zero power against a global Rz sign convention fault
  over the gate family {H, X, Z, Ry, Rz, CX, CZ}: 0 of 16418 non-equivalent
  mutants, 95% Wilson interval [0.000%, 0.023%]. Inverting a single Rz is a
  different fault class and is ordinarily visible, which the earlier literature
  conflates.

  Second, in quantum error correction, an oracle that derives its reference from
  the circuit under test has zero power against a deterministically inserted
  Pauli: 0 of 321 for X and 0 of 211 for Z on a rotated surface code, while
  detecting every other injected fault class at 100%. A four-instruction witness
  exhibits two circuits with identical detector error models, identical
  self-referential detection rates, and cross-referential rates of 0 and 1.

  Power throughout is measured against non-equivalent mutants only, with the
  reference oracle validated by a known-answer battery before use.
keywords:
  - quantum computing
  - differential testing
  - mutation testing
  - test oracle
  - quantum error correction
  - compiler correctness
  - Rust
license: Apache-2.0
